\details{
\strong{Method}
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Neighbor graph.} Each sample is linked to its \code{n_neighbors} nearest
other samples (or to every sample within \code{radius}), by an edge as long
as the distance between them. The graph is undirected: a link from
either end counts. Ties are broken by sample order, so that tied
distances and duplicate samples (distance 0) give a well-defined graph.
\item \strong{Joining components.} If the graph falls apart into several
connected components, as it often does for clustered data, every pair
of components is joined by one edge between their two closest samples,
with a warning. The joined graph's geodesics between clusters then run
through those closest pairs. Raise \code{n_neighbors} to avoid this. If
there are so many components that joining them would add more than 10
times as many edges as the neighbor graph has (and more than 65,536),
\code{isomap()} stops with an error instead (see "Speed and memory").
\item \strong{Geodesic distances.} The length of the shortest path through the
graph between every pair of samples, by Dijkstra's algorithm, one
search per sample (scikit-learn lets SciPy choose between Dijkstra
and Floyd-Warshall; both give the same lengths, up to rounding).
\item \strong{Embedding.} Classical scaling of the geodesic distances, exactly as
in \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}: the top \code{k} eigenvectors of
\eqn{B = -\frac{1}{2}JG^{(2)}J}, where \eqn{G^{(2)}} holds the squared
geodesic distances and \eqn{J} is the centering matrix, scaled by the
square roots of their eigenvalues. Axes whose eigenvalues aren't
positive are dropped with a warning, as in \code{pcoa()}.
}

With \code{n_neighbors} = \eqn{n - 1}, every pair of samples is linked, and
when \code{dm} obeys the triangle inequality (e.g. Euclidean distances), the
geodesic distances are \code{dm} itself, so \code{isomap()} gives the same result
as \code{pcoa()}. Small \code{n_neighbors} follow the data's shape more closely but
are more easily split into components and thrown off by noise.

\strong{Speed and memory}

Neighbors are found straight from \code{dm}, in parallel. The geodesic
distances are held as one more packed lower triangle the size of \code{dm}
(8 bytes per pair of samples: 10 GB for 50,000 samples), which is the
only large allocation; an \eqn{n \times n} matrix is never made. They
are kept in double precision, as \code{dm} is, since the eigenvectors need
it. Each search computes only the distances from its sample to later
samples, so each pair is computed once; searches run in parallel, \code{cpus}
at a time. The eigenvectors come from block Lanczos iteration, as in
\code{pcoa()}, which needs a few dozen vectors of length \eqn{n}. Besides
that, the graph takes about \code{44 * n * n_neighbors} bytes (or 32 bytes
per linked pair of samples with \code{radius}), and each thread 28 bytes per
sample.

With \eqn{E} edges (about \code{n * n_neighbors} to twice that), the time is
about \eqn{n^2} to find neighbors, plus \eqn{n (n + E) \log n} for the
searches, plus a few dozen passes over the geodesic distances for the
eigenvectors, all divided among \code{cpus}.

Joining \eqn{c > 1} components takes one more pass over \code{dm}, adds
\eqn{c (c - 1) / 2} edges (32 bytes each), and needs a table of 16 bytes
per pair of components for each thread that scans for the closest pairs
(one thread at least; more only while all their tables fit in 16 MB).
Every search may relax every joining edge, so a small \code{radius} or
\code{n_neighbors} that leaves many samples on their own would make memory
grow with \eqn{c^2} and time with \eqn{n c^2}. To keep both within a
small multiple of what a connected graph needs, \code{isomap()} refuses (with
an error saying how many components it found) when the joining edges
would number more than 10 times the neighbor graph's own edges, and more
than 65,536. That floor (about 3 MB) lets small datasets join freely: up
to 362 samples can all be on their own.

\strong{Differences from scikit-learn}

\code{isomap()} gives the same geodesic distances (\code{dist_matrix_}), up to
rounding, and the same embedding (\code{embedding_}) and eigenvalues as
\code{sklearn.manifold.Isomap(metric = "precomputed")}, except:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Ties.} scikit-learn chooses among neighbors tied in distance by
\code{numpy.argpartition()}, in an order that isn't specified, and so can
link different ones among duplicate samples too. \code{isomap()} always
prefers the earlier sample. When no tie straddles the
\code{n_neighbors}-th neighbor, the two graphs are the same.
\item \strong{Axes with eigenvalues that aren't positive.} scikit-learn stops
with an error when one of the top \code{k} eigenvalues is substantially
negative, and otherwise returns axes of zeros for them. \code{isomap()}
drops them with a warning, as \code{pcoa()} does.
\item \strong{Rounding.} Each geodesic distance is summed along its path from the
earlier of its two samples. SciPy may sum the same path from the other
end, or with Floyd-Warshall's grouping, which can differ in the last
bit or two. The eigenvectors come from block Lanczos rather than LAPACK
or ARPACK. In the package's tests, geodesic distances agree to within
\eqn{4 \times 10^{-16}} (relative), and coordinates to within
\eqn{10^{-12}} of the largest one.
\item \strong{\code{n_neighbors} larger than \eqn{n - 1}.} scikit-learn stops with an
error; \code{isomap()} uses \eqn{n - 1}, with a warning.
\item \strong{Very many components.} scikit-learn joins any number of them;
\code{isomap()} refuses past the limit above.
\item \strong{Sign.} scikit-learn flips each axis so that its largest coordinate
in absolute value is positive. \code{isomap()} does as \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} does: it
makes positive the first coordinate whose absolute value is within a
relative \eqn{10^{-8}} of the largest, so that rounding can't break a
tie differently on different computers. The two only differ when an
axis's largest absolute values are tied, or nearly so, with opposite
signs.
